<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venetian Dalmatia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1420">1420</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> was the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s possession on the eastern Adriatic coast, held from 1420, when Venice bought the Hungarian claim and took over most coastal towns, until the fall of the republic in 1797. Governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>, it supplied sailors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and soldiers. The wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> extended it inland in the late 17th and early 18th centuries; it then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venetian-dalmatia.jpg" alt="Venetian Dalmatia" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venezianische_Kolonien.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of the coast for the eastern Adriatic since the 7th and 8th centuries. Around 1000 an expedition under Doge Pietro II Orseolo secured Venetian suzerainty over coastal Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, and he named himself Duke of Dalmatia. The coastal cities then changed hands repeatedly between Venice, the Croatian kings and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>; the crusaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> for Venice in 1202, and by the Treaty of Zadar of 18 February 1358 Venice lost the whole coast to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><p>During the Hungarian civil war between King Sigismund and the House of Anjou, the losing contender, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a>, sold his rights on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for 100,000 ducats. Sigismund tried to recover the territory, but Venice defeated his troops at the Battle of Motta in 1412. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, as the Republic of Ragusa, stayed outside Venetian rule, and the southernmost part of the coast was known as Venetian Albania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Màr</a>. Its cities were run by magistrates under the Provveditore Generale in Dalmazia e Albania, who usually sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>. In the Ottoman–Venetian wars that began in 1499 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> took the Croatian lands of the hinterland between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Skradin</a> and Obrovac, without taking the Venetian cities. Dalmatians served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> as sailors, oltramarini infantry, cappelletti cavalry and militia: more than 9,000 were in the Venetian fleet at Lepanto.</p><p>In the Cretan War the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Novigrad in 1646, but the Venetian commander Leonardo Foscolo retook it and seized Klis, and the peace of 1669 left Venetian territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> three times larger. In the Morean War Venice took Sinj on 30 September 1686, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a> in 1687 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a> on 11 September 1688, which fixed much of the later border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> added Sinj and Imotski along the line named after Sebastiano Mocenigo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the late 18th century the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> had come to mean only the Venetian territory. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> dissolved the republic in 1797, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> annexed its Dalmatian lands. The area passed to the French client <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1805 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> in 1809, and after Napoleon's defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> made it the Kingdom of Dalmatia in 1815.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dalmatia under Venetian rule” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its principal danger came from Venice, which sought to dominate the Adriatic, and the Treaty of Karlowitz of 1699 left two narrow strips of Ottoman territory at Neum and Sutorina, separating Ragusa from <b>Venetian Dalmatia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Austria had taken <b>Venetian Dalmatia</b> in 1797 but lost it to Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dalmatia">Kingdom of Dalmatia</a></li></ul></section>
